<PageLayout title='Genomic Architecture Project' kicker='PROJECT // ACTIVE RESEARCH' lead='How many evolutionary histories can coexist inside one genome?'>
<section><div class="prose content-page"><div class="note"><strong>Working research.</strong> The results described here are preliminary analytical outputs and have not yet been peer-reviewed or published.</div><p>This project uses <em>Steinernema carpocapsae</em> as a working system to investigate variation in coalescent history across the nuclear genome and its relationship to the mitochondrial timescale.</p><p>The project originally tested a more specific mitonuclear hypothesis: if nuclear regions share coalescent timing with the mitochondrial genome, are those regions enriched for mitochondrial functions? Initial analyses did not reveal such enrichment. Instead of ending the project, that result shifted the question toward the organization of local evolutionary histories across the genome.</p><h2>$ working_dataset</h2><p>Current analyses include mitochondrial variation across seven focal individuals plus an outgroup, nuclear TMRCA summaries across genomic windows, chromosome-level comparisons, gene overlap and functional annotation. A working screen retained roughly two million nuclear-window observations and merged them into thousands of candidate regions with mitochondrial-like coalescent timing.</p><h2>$ current_direction</h2><ul><li>Classify genomic regions into recent, intermediate and deep TMRCA groups.</li><li>Test whether these regimes form non-random chromosomal blocks.</li><li>Compare functional content and genomic context across regimes.</li><li>Distinguish biological signal from windowing, annotation and mapping artefacts.</li><li>Develop figures that show the genome as a mosaic of local histories.</li></ul><div class="quote">The failed enrichment hypothesis opened a larger question: what organizes coalescent time across a genome?</div></div></section>
</PageLayout>